We can rewrite this equation as \[ f(2x + 1) - f(3x - 1) = 2x^2 - 5x + 1 - f(x).\] We then set $y=2x$ to get \[ f(x) + f(4x + 1) + 10x^2 = f(5x - 1) + 8x^2 + 1.\] We can rewrite this equation as \[ f(4x + 1) - f(5x - 1) = 7x^2 - f(x).\] We can repeat this process for other values of $x$ and $y$ to obtain multiple equations relating $f(x)$ for different values of $x$. By adding these equations together, we can eliminate $f(3x - 1)$ and $f(5x - 1)$, and use $f(2x + 1)$ and $f(4x + 1)$ to express $f(x)$ in terms of $x$ alone. We can then determine the value of $f(10)$ by plugging in $x=10$ into this expression. The answer is: \boxed{9}

